<urn:atm:triage:finding/SMK-003>
  a triage:Finding ;
  triage:findingId "SMK-003" ;
  triage:title "One unresolvable trusted-peer entry aborts unrelated literal-IP sends" ;
  triage:description "fenix (ATM 01M2ANEESW1KSCS1J697J8E8MA, 2026-09-12T11:18:37Z), found during 1.5.16 local smoke (local-ip lane) on rand-m5. A stale `atm-hermes-testbed.local` trusted-peer entry (leftover of a run.sh PEER-mode start; no /etc/hosts mapping for it) made every literal-IP send fail with \"trusted peer ... could not be resolved\" -- one unresolvable trust entry aborted sends to unrelated, resolvable IP targets. fenix revoked the stale entry (test.sh does not use peer mode; run.sh re-adds it) and added `rand-m5.local` (own fingerprint, port 43101) so the local-ip lane has a peer that resolves to 192.168.1.155, unblocking the smoke run. Product question for a design ruling: should an unresolvable trusted peer be skipped (with a warning) at send time rather than aborting the send outright? Recorded as Important pending a ruling; workaround (revoke the stale entry) is in place and does not block 1.5.16 shipping." ;
  triage:phaseId "phase-ba" ;
  triage:triageMode "initial_pass" ;
  triage:category "hardening" ;
  triage:severity "important" ;
  triage:source "qa" ;
  triage:repeatable true ;
  triage:sweepScope "file_only" ;
  triage:status "open" ;
  triage:dispatchReady false ;
  triage:triagedAt "2026-09-12T11:18:37Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
  triage:deferredNote "Recorded as reversible host trust state, not a code defect in itself: the workaround (revoke the stale atm-hermes-testbed.local entry, add rand-m5.local) unblocked the smoke run. Open item is the product design question -- should resolve-failure on one trusted peer be a per-send warning-and-skip instead of an abort -- pending a design ruling before dispatch." ;
  triage:foundIn triage:BA6 ;
  triage:foundAt "2026-09-12T11:18:37Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
  triage:hasOccurrence <urn:atm:triage:occurrence/SMK-003/1> ;
  triage:triageRecordVersion "1" .

